  "derivation": "Starting from E = 2(1+u)g + g^2 - r with g = r/2 + r^2*B + u*r*C, I solved for B and C that make E identically zero. The exact increment satisfying (1+u+g)^2 = (1+u)^2 + r is g_exact = r/((1+u)*(sqrt(1+r/(1+u)^2)+1)). Writing g_exact - r/2 = -u*r/(2(1+u)) + O(r^2) identifies C = -1/(2(1+u)) to cancel the O(r) term. The remaining O(r^2) part is -r^2/(2(1+u)^3*(sqrt(1+r/(1+u)^2)+1)^2), giving B. Direct algebra confirms (1+u+g)^2 = (1+u)^2 + r exactly, using (s-1)(s+1) = r/(1+u)^2 with s = sqrt(1+r/(1+u)^2). At u=0 this reduces to the source-only formula B = -1/(2*(1+sqrt(1+r))^2), C=0. The formula is C-infinity on an open neighbourhood of the closed rectangle |r|<=1/2, |u|<=1/4 because 1+u >= 3/4 and 1+r/(1+u)^2 >= 7/9 there, keeping the square-root argument strictly positive.",
